<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-06">
  <title>Week 6: Convergence of Measurable Functions (2E) </title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Littlewood">
    <title>Littlewood's Three Principles</title>

    <p>
        Recall the definitions of pointwise and uniform convergence.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-pointwise-convergence">
        <title>Pointwise and Uniform Convergence</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose $X$ is a set, $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ are functions from $X$ to $\mathbf{R}$ and $f$ is a function from $X$ to $\mathbf{R}$. 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The sequence $f_1, f_2, \ldots $ converges pointwise on $X$ to $f$ if 
                        <md>
                            \lim_{k\to \infty} f_k(x) = f(x)
                        </md>
                        for each $x \in X$. In other words, $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ converges pointwise on $X$ to $f$ if for each $x \in X$ and every $\epsilon &gt; 0$ there exists $n \in \mathbf{N}$ such that $|f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \epsilon$ for all integers $k \geq n$.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The sequence $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ converges uniformly on $X$ to $f$ if for every $\epsilon &gt; 0$ there exists $n \in \mathbf{N}$ such that $|f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \epsilon$ for all integers $k \geq n$ and all $x \in X$.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Example: Let $g_n(x) = x^n$ on $[0,1]$. Then $g(x)$ converges pointwise to 
        <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill 0 \amp \text{ if $ 0 \leq x &lt; 1$} \\
      \hfill  1 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x = 1$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        However, $g_n(x)$ does not converge uniformly on $[0,1]$.
    </p>

    <p>
        The previous example shows that the pointwise limit of a sequence of continuous functions need not be continuous; however, the uniform limit of a sequence of continuous functions will always be continuous.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-unfiform-limit-continuous">
        <title>Uniform Limit of a Sequence of Continuous Function is Continuous</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose $B \subseteq \mathbf{R}$ and $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of functions from $B$ to $\mathbf{R}$. Suppose $b \in B$ and $f_k$ is continuous at $b$ for each $k \in \mathbf{R}$. Then $f$ is continuous at $B$.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        The following propositions are clearly false.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable set is a finite union of intervals.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable function is continuous.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every pointwise convergent sequence is uniformly convergent.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        However, it only takes a slight modification to change them into true statements. These are called <term>Littlewood's Three Principles</term>. The following are true statements.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measureable set is <em>nearly</em> a finite union of intervals.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable function is <em>nearly</em> continuous.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every pointwise convergent sequence is <em>nearly</em> uniformly convergent.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    
    <p>
        Here <em>nearly</em> means that the statement holds outside a set with arbitrarily small measure.
    </p>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Egorov">
    <title>Egorov's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        We essentially proved (a) in our theorem of equivalences of being a Lebesgue measurable set. Next we will prove (c).
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Egorov">
        <title>Egorov's Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose $(X,S,\mu)$ is a measure space with $\mu(X) &lt; \infty$. Suppose $f_1,f_2, \ldots$ is a sequence of $S$-measurable functions from $X$ to $\mathbf{R}$ that converges pointwise on $X$ to a function $f:X \to \mathbf{R}$. Then for every $\epsilon &gt; 0$ there exists a set $E \in S$ such that $\mu(X \setminus E) &lt; \epsilon$ and $f_1,f_2,\ldots$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $X$.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        Example: Consider the sequence $g_n(x) = x^n$, which is a sequence of Lebesgue measurable functions from $X = [0,1]$ to $\mathbf{R}$ which converges pointwise to
        <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill 0 \amp \text{ if $ 0 \leq x &lt; 1$} \\
      \hfill  1 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x = 1$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        
    </p>

    <p>
        Given $\epsilon &gt; 0$, let $E = [0, 1-\frac{\epsilon}{2}]$. Then $\mu(X \setminus E) = \frac{\epsilon}{2} &lt; \epsilon$ and $g_n(x)$ converges uniformly to $g(x)$ on $E$.
    </p>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose $\epsilon &gt; 0$ and temporarily fix $n \in \mathbf{N}$. Because $\{f_n\}$ converges pointwise to $f$, we have that
            <md>
                \bigcup_{m=1}^{\infty} \bigcap_{k=m}^{\infty} \left\{x\in X: |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt;  \frac{1}{n}\right\} = X
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
           For $m \in \mathbf{N}$ let 
           <md>
            A_{m,n} = \bigcap_{k=m}^{\infty} \left\{x\in X: |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \frac{1}{n}\right\}.
           </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Because each $f_k$ is an $S$-measurable function $f$ is an $S$-measurable function. Hence each $f_k-f$ is an $S$-measurable function and $A_{m,n} \in S$.
        </p>

        <p>
            Since $A_{1,n} \subseteq A_{2,n} \subseteq \ldots$ is an increasing sequence of sets and 
            <md>
                \bigcup_{m=1}^{\infty} A_{m,n} = X
            </md>
            it follows that $\displaystyle \lim_{m\to \infty} \mu(A_{m,n}) = \mu(X)$.
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus there exists $m_n \in \mathbf{N}$ such that $\mu(X) - \mu(A_{m_n,n}) &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2^n}$.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now let $E = \displaystyle \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_{m_n,n}$. Then
            <md>
                <mrow> \mu(X \setminus E) \amp \left( X \setminus \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_{m_n,n} \right) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \mu \left( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} X \setminus A_{m_n,n} \right)</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp \leq \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(X \setminus A_{m_n,n})</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp \leq \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(X) - \mu(A_{m_n,n}) = \epsilon. </mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            To complete the proof, we need to show that the sequence $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ converges to $f$ uniformly on $E$. Suppose $\epsilon' &gt; 0$ and let $n \in \mathbf{N}$ be such that $\frac{1}{n} &lt; \epsilon'$. Then because $E \subseteq A_{m_n,n}$ we have that
            <md>
                |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \frac{1}{n} &lt; \epsilon'
            </md>
             for all $k \geq m_n$ and $x \in E$. Hence $f_1, f_2, \ldots$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $E$.
        </p>

    </proof>
    
    
  </subsection>


  </section>